    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To differentiate between a structured, periodic nuisance parameter and a series of non-periodic but non-random stochastic shocks, one can examine the power spectral density (PSD) of the residuals. The PSD is a function that describes the distribution of power across different frequencies in the time series.\n\nFor a purely random process, the PSD is typically characterized by a white noise spectrum, which is a flat function of frequency, often represented as σ^2 / (2πf). In contrast, a structured, periodic nuisance parameter will exhibit a periodic pattern in the PSD, which can be described by a Fourier series.\n\nMore specifically, if the nuisance parameter is periodic with period T, the PSD will exhibit a peak at frequencies k/T, where k is an integer. This peak will be characterized by a Lorentzian shape, with a half-width at half-maximum (HWFM) of approximately 1/T.\n\nOn the other hand, a series of non-periodic but non-random stochastic shocks will not exhibit a periodic pattern in the PSD. Instead, the PSD will be characterized by a series of peaks at different frequencies, each corresponding to a different shock. These peaks will be characterized by a Gaussian distribution, with a standard deviation that depends on the amplitude of",
